.TH MS 1 "December 2025" "1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ms \- list directory contents with advanced formatting options
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ms
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATH\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ms
is a modern replacement for the traditional
.B ls
command with enhanced formatting options and display modes.
.PP
Without options,
.B ms
lists the files in the current directory in alphabetical order.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help information and exit.
.TP
.B \-l
List in long format, showing file permissions, owner, group, size, and modification time.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
List all files, including hidden files (those beginning with '.').
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-recursive
List directories recursively.
.TP
.B \-k
Display directory contents in tree format.
.TP
.B \-K
Display directory contents in tree format with metadata (permissions, size, modification time).
.TP
.B \-n
Display contents in table format with filename and file size columns.
.TP
.B \-N
Display contents in 2-column row format.
.TP
.B \-z
Display contents in zebra-striped list view with alternating row colors.
.TP
.B \-Z
Display contents in zebra-striped list view with metadata columns.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-human\-readable
Display file sizes in human-readable format (K, M, G, etc.).
.TP
.B \-t
Display contents in horizontal table format (2xN layout).
.TP
.B \-T
Display contents in horizontal table format with metadata.
.TP
.B \-d
Show detailed information about each file.
.TP
.B \-\-sort=\fISORT_BY\fR
Sort files by specified criteria:
.RS
.TP
.B name
Sort alphabetically by filename (default).
.TP
.B size
Sort by file size.
.TP
.B time
Sort by modification time.
.TP
.B type
Sort by file type.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse the sort order.
.SH DISPLAY MODES
.SS Tree View (\-k, \-K)
The tree view displays directory contents in a hierarchical tree structure:
.PP
.B \-k
Displays simple tree structure.
.PP
.B \-K
Displays tree structure with metadata including permissions, size, and modification time.
.SS Table Modes (\-n, \-t, \-T)
Table modes display files in organized columns:
.PP
.B \-n
Two-column table showing filename and size.
.PP
.B \-t
Horizontal table layout (2xN) showing filenames only.
.PP
.B \-T
Horizontal table layout with metadata (size, permissions).
.SS Zebra List (\-z, \-Z)
Zebra list modes display files with alternating row colors for better readability:
.PP
.B \-z
Zebra striped list with filename only.
.PP
.B \-Z
Zebra striped list with metadata columns.
.SH SORTING
Files can be sorted by different criteria using the
.B \-\-sort
option:
.IP
.B \-\-sort=name
Sort alphabetically by filename (default behavior).
.IP
.B \-\-sort=size
Sort by file size, smallest first.
.IP
.B \-\-sort=time
Sort by modification time, newest first.
.IP
.B \-\-sort=type
Sort by file type (directories first, then regular files, etc.).
.PP
The
.B \-\-reverse
option reverses any sort order.
.SH HUMAN READABLE SIZES
When the
.B \-H
option is used, file sizes are displayed in a more readable format:
.IP
.B 1023 bytes
or less: displayed as bytes
.IP
.B 1024 bytes
to 1 MB: displayed as K (kilobytes)
.IP
.B 1 MB
to 1 GB: displayed as M (megabytes)
.IP
.B 1 GB
to 1 TB: displayed as G (gigabytes)
.IP
.B 1 TB
and above: displayed as T (terabytes)
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ms
List files in current directory.
.TP
.B ms \-l /home/user
List files in /home/user in long format.
.TP
.B ms \-k /usr/bin
Display /usr/bin contents in tree format.
.TP
.B ms \-n \-H .
Display current directory in table format with human-readable sizes.
.TP
.B ms \-z \-R
Display current directory recursively with zebra striping.
.TP
.B ms \-\-sort=size \-\-reverse \-l
List files sorted by size (largest first) in long format.
.TP
.B ms \-T \-H /var/log
Display /var/log in horizontal table with metadata and human-readable sizes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to determine the user's home directory.
.TP
.B PWD
Current working directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
General error or file access problem.
.SH AUTHOR
Lotuschain_org Agent
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ls (1),
.BR tree (1),
.BR find (1)
.SH NOTES
.PP
The
.B ms
command is designed as a modern replacement for
.B ls
with enhanced visual formatting options.
.PP
Some display modes may not work properly on all terminal emulators.
.PP
Zebra list modes use ANSI color codes and may not display correctly on all systems.
.SH BUGS
.PP
Report bugs to the system administrator.
.PP
The command may behave differently on various Unix-like systems due to filesystem differences.